    "params": {
        "video_subject": "4 · Why old money whispers",
        "video_script": "Number four: why old money whispers. Marketing researchers looked at luxury handbags from brands like Louis Vuitton and Gucci, and sorted them by how loudly they show the logo. Some have the brand printed all over. Others have a tiny logo, or none you'd spot. The quiet ones were, on average, the more expensive bags. Then they looked at who buys what. People with lots of money and no need to prove it preferred the quiet versions. They signal to a small group of insiders who recognise the design without a logo. People with money who want to be seen as rich preferred the loud versions. And people who want status but can't afford it were the most likely to buy counterfeits, almost always of the loud kind. The researchers called the quiet group patricians. Old money. Your brain reads a giant logo as 'look at me', and the people at the very top know it. So looking rich has a strange rule: the more you have, the less you need to show. That's why a watch with no visible logo can say more, to the right people, than one covered in diamonds. Try this today: think of the last thing you bought partly so others would notice. Would a quieter version have done the job? But if loud signals aren't the top of the ladder, what is? The next study found something stranger: breaking the rules.",
